{"title":"2016 USAMO Problems/Problem 2","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Controversial)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Controversial)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Induction,_Incorrect?)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Induction, Incorrect?)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_(Structural_Proof_without_polynomials)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 (Structural Proof without polynomials)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Prove that for any positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/9/c99a6aaebc17474bd09ea71d84d4e769a0662b55.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"17\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/d/4ed23f598fe1573fe79161056cfcad1ffcb0f1fd.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\left(k^2\\right)!\\cdot\\prod_{j=0}^{k-1}\\frac{j!}{\\left(j+k\\right)!}\\]\" width=\"138\" height=\"52\" />\nis an integer.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Note that the expression that we're trying to prove is an integer, call it <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, is rational.\n</p><p>We will show that, for every prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=P-adic_valuation\" title=\"P-adic valuation\">p-adic valuation</a> of n, written as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/4/c14c93f3a73758bb4731d45d5c9ca054120216b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_p(N)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"45\" height=\"18\" /> is non-negative. This implies that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is an integer.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/6/e06bb569ced9799855e20274551906d832317322.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_p(N)=\\sum_{i=1}^\\infty \\left\\lfloor \\frac{k^{2}}{p^{i}} \\right\\rfloor+\\sum_{j=0}^{k-1} \\sum_{i=1}^\\infty \\left\\lfloor \\frac{j}{p^{i}}\\right\\rfloor-\\sum_{j=k}^{2k-1} \\sum_{i=1}^\\infty \\left\\lfloor \\frac{j}{p^{i}} \\right\\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -26px\" width=\"387\" height=\"59\" />, by <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Legendre%27s_Formula\" title=\"Legendre&#39;s Formula\">Legendre's Formula</a>.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/f/33f54ffc775f61761b4c55d7183121ea803ec91f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left\\lfloor{\\frac{x}{p}}\\right\\rfloor={\\frac{x-(x \\mod p)}{p}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"184\" height=\"45\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/2/7f2aacf15476c09b29acb538fb63fa2a2b0b1b88.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{i=0}^{k-1} (i \\mod m)\\leq \\sum_{i=k}^{2k-1} (i \\mod m)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"261\" height=\"56\" />. (We take out groups of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> which are cyclic permutations of numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> until there are less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> left, then we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> distinct values, and the minimum possible sum of leftovers is attained on the left hand side as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/c/e1c12f6448225e03b3246ef831c10ae3265b689c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{i=0}^{(k -1) \\mod m} i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"96\" height=\"58\" />).\n</p><p>Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/f/eafe2029c22e56fd9bcb4ed77fe4a142c20990c3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_p(N)=\\sum_{m=p^{i}, i\\in \\mathbb{N}_{+}}-\\frac{k^{2}}{m}+\\left\\lfloor{\\frac{k^{2}}{m}}\\right\\rfloor-\\frac{\\sum_{i=0}^{k-1} r(i,m)-\\sum_{i=k}^{2k-1} r(i,m)}{m} \\geq \\sum_{m=p^{i}, i\\in \\mathbb{N}} \\left\\lceil -\\frac{k^{2}}{m}+\\lfloor{\\frac{k^{2}}{m}}\\rfloor\\right\\rceil \\geq 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -24px\" width=\"736\" height=\"54\" />, as the term in each summand is a sum of floors also and is clearly an integer.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Controversial.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Controversial)\">Solution 2 (Controversial)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Controversial)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Consider an <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/e/97e11746bde904a399f341e9c7ee8f328c526c8f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k\\times k$\" width=\"42\" height=\"12\" /> grid, which is to be filled with the integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/5/0e5c83cc0c639fc2ed843bbe2d3d209fb478b104.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"17\" /> such that the numbers in each row are in increasing order from left to right, and such that the numbers in each column are in increasing order from bottom to top. In other words, we are creating an <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/e/97e11746bde904a399f341e9c7ee8f328c526c8f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k\\times k$\" width=\"42\" height=\"12\" /> standard Young tableaux.\n</p><p>The Hook Length Formula is the source of the controversy, as it is very powerful and trivializes this problem. The Hook Length Formula states that the number of ways to create this standard Young tableaux (call this <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> for convenience) is:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/f/b3f62bc17757c391a8f65ec8b19ee22e7f815366.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[N = \\frac{\\left(k^2\\right)!}{\\prod_{1\\le i, j\\le k}(i+j-1)}.\\]\" width=\"197\" height=\"46\" />\nNow, we do some simple rearrangement:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/8/c6855e715e5eda8aac9d40a98b9d04cfb3b3f182.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[N = \\left(k^2\\right)!\\cdot\\prod_{j=1}^{k}\\prod_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{i+j-1} = \\left(k^2\\right)!\\cdot\\prod_{j=1}^{k}\\frac{\\left(j-1\\right)!}{\\left(j+k-1\\right)!}\\]\" width=\"412\" height=\"52\" /> \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/e/79ea7074962305377f90f92c06fa4b74bd6f4980.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[= \\left(k^2\\right)!\\cdot\\prod_{j=0}^{k-1}\\frac{j!}{\\left(j+k\\right)!}.\\]\" width=\"165\" height=\"52\" />\nThis is exactly the expression given in the problem! Since the expression given in the problem equals the number of distinct <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/e/97e11746bde904a399f341e9c7ee8f328c526c8f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k\\times k$\" width=\"42\" height=\"12\" /> standard Young tableaux, it must be an integer, so we are done.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Induction.2C_Incorrect.3F.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Induction,_Incorrect?)\">Solution 3 (Induction, Incorrect?)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Induction, Incorrect?)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><b>Note: This is incorrect. (Why?)</b>\n</p><p><br />\nDefine <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/6/3d6e8a0d850b4bc706e005f6794ec20df15e4450.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[A(k) = \\left(k^2\\right)!\\cdot\\prod_{j=0}^{k-1}\\frac{j!}{\\left(j+k\\right)!}.\\]\" width=\"208\" height=\"52\" /> Clearly, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/c/eac12db1d1ceea51aacb82a04a90cbd5a971e443.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A(1) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"69\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/f/14f2bf4e196102331a8eb0c92935056714e80012.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A(2) = 2.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"73\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/c/82c06873583b016ac8625bc339e88b7081804d0e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{A(k+1)}{A(k)} = \\frac{\\left(k^2+2k+1\\right)!\\cdot\\prod_{j=0}^{k}\\frac{j!}{\\left(j+k+1\\right)!}}{\\left(k^2\\right)!\\cdot\\prod_{j=0}^{k-1}\\frac{j!}{\\left(j+k\\right)!}}.\\]\" width=\"327\" height=\"58\" /> Lots of terms cancel, and we are left with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/0/600d6f50747a247cc7ff50e30366c5876c4245be.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{A(k+1)}{A(k)} = \\frac{(k^2+1)(k^2+2)\\cdots(k^2+2k+1)}{2(2k+1)}.\\]\" width=\"360\" height=\"43\" /> The numerator has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/7/f2789508227865dfc89fd46e18ae9b25bff34257.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2k+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"49\" height=\"14\" /> consecutive positive integers, so one of them must be divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/1/de1cf92825b361fc5922d0ad5a2bef386c8a9769.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2k+1).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"68\" height=\"18\" /> Also, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/e/b8ea7ee8a282949fd853a01537ec7a2b55e56f75.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2k$\" width=\"18\" height=\"12\" /> terms left, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> of which are even. We can choose one of these to cancel out the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> in the denominator. Therefore, the ratio between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/d/2/dd2f6376f262030397b6d4f8923ba129f7421b67.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A(k+1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"67\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/6/906b024d306b436141b65697d0cbc59625f615ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A(k)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"36\" height=\"18\" /> is an integer. By our inductive hypothesis, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/6/906b024d306b436141b65697d0cbc59625f615ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A(k)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"36\" height=\"18\" /> is an integer. Therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/d/2/dd2f6376f262030397b6d4f8923ba129f7421b67.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A(k+1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"67\" height=\"18\" /> is as well, and we are done.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let us work in the ring of polynomials <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/1/8f1bdee6d882efd9a8646551ccccea2439f7fe6e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{Z}[q]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"33\" height=\"20\" />.  Define\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/e/d2e1ca410dd9ef98847a5b7c14d758ffcefc7287.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[[n]_q = 1 + q + \\cdots + q^{n-1}, \\qquad [n]_q! = \\prod_{i=1}^n [i]_q.\\]\" width=\"340\" height=\"47\" />\nSince\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/8/4486b6c96ac14b92f00406820fa85625c61336ca.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[[n]_q! = \\prod_{i=1}^n\\frac{1 - q^i}{1 - q} = \\prod_{d=1}^n \\frac{\\Phi_d(q)^{\\lfloor n/d\\rfloor}}{1 - q},\\]\" width=\"268\" height=\"49\" />\nwe have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/d/c6d67a7c9540614f099d38591ab9b7cc83d09473.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\nu_d\\bigl([n]_q!\\bigr) = \\lfloor n/d\\rfloor.\\]\" width=\"139\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p>Set\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/5/d3552b349688f1f8e1de9113c7ba29d4496ba609.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[P_k(q) = [k^2]_q!\\,\\prod_{j=0}^{k-1}\\frac{[j]_q!}{[j+k]_q!}.\\]\" width=\"210\" height=\"53\" />\nThen\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/1/cc13b3e23eb55d469972faec7cfdb83d7ae6d63a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\nu_1\\bigl(P_k(q)\\bigr) = 0\\]\" width=\"107\" height=\"21\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/c/16c255781d3baecab3d16232786d772255ebf915.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\nu_d\\bigl(P_k(q)\\bigr) = \\lfloor k^2/d\\rfloor + \\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}\\lfloor j/d\\rfloor - \\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}\\lfloor (j+k)/d\\rfloor.\\]\" width=\"383\" height=\"53\" />\nCombine the last two sums:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/c/e3c04420b206fc8373ab01a52470b25dd39a5e0e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}\\lfloor j/d\\rfloor - \\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}\\lfloor (j+k)/d\\rfloor = -\\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}\\Bigl(\\lfloor (j+k)/d\\rfloor - \\lfloor j/d\\rfloor\\Bigr).\\]\" width=\"453\" height=\"53\" />\nHence\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/c/10cd2842bb49a299087454cb00d91ce91e6bb96a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\nu_d\\bigl(P_k(q)\\bigr) = \\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k^2}{d}\\Big\\rfloor - \\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}\\Bigl(\\lfloor\\tfrac{j+k}{d}\\rfloor - \\lfloor\\tfrac{j}{d}\\rfloor\\Bigr).\\]\" width=\"305\" height=\"53\" />\nBut for each <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/6/c96700d3e95188bd892aafa496e4b98a80eae1d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0\\le j&lt;k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"78\" height=\"18\" />, the difference <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/1/0d175b6b67c46032f55a1466e2aaf93b54172347.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lfloor\\tfrac{j+k}{d}\\rfloor - \\lfloor\\tfrac{j}{d}\\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"95\" height=\"24\" /> counts the multiples of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ab646de7704969b91c76a214126b45f2b07b25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> in the interval <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/8/d4817533b2e6f6bf15107faf0cb33238d3e0edec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(j,j+k]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"70\" height=\"20\" />.  As <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/2/8122aa89ea6e80784c6513d22787ad86e36ad0cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"10\" height=\"17\" /> runs from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/c/7dc6fab4c2fdb4e9046030fbfdcf6ea721b05602.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" />, these <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> intervals lie inside <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/b/93b1ae22ff70aacfdd123e1c1b0a8d43ba8ef06c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1,2,\\dots,k^2\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"104\" height=\"21\" />, so\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c30ff6794171cbb8168d984ef970177fd33b28f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}\\Bigl(\\lfloor\\tfrac{j+k}{d}\\rfloor - \\lfloor\\tfrac{j}{d}\\rfloor\\Bigr) \\;\\le\\; \\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k^2}{d}\\Big\\rfloor.\\]\" width=\"217\" height=\"53\" />\nIt follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/e/2fe787744dd6180037a0adbda753544ab788fae4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\nu_d(P_k(q))\\ge0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"109\" height=\"20\" />\n, hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/a/d9a8114b3b9a93347f393fd27f2627207a73b605.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_k(q)\\in\\mathbb{Z}[q]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"98\" height=\"20\" />.  Evaluating at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/5/26520f2333a3d48eb4929b7517dea54293f36605.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"44\" height=\"17\" /> completes the proof.\n</p><p>~Lopkiloinm\n</p><p>Note: This solution is fundamentally different from the first, which works purely in the integers. Here, we work in the integer polynomial ring <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/1/8f1bdee6d882efd9a8646551ccccea2439f7fe6e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{Z}[q]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"33\" height=\"20\" />—a graded algebra—which allows us to test integrality by tracking the multiplicities of better organized elements like <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/9/959fd98c9844b4a863d3b3877c8fc19360358d00.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Phi_d(q)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"46\" height=\"20\" /> for all integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/7/dc7acd76b3411f0ceeed8a01052d9bf11febcf2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\le d \\le k^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"86\" height=\"19\" />. In contrast, working in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/0/d4023589a374e5b4a1bc6063d887aec205e0d3e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />—a non-graded algebra—requires using <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />-adic valuations via Legendre’s formula, which only considers primes which are less organized. The graded structure of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/1/8f1bdee6d882efd9a8646551ccccea2439f7fe6e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{Z}[q]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"33\" height=\"20\" /> simplifies the analysis, making it a powerful strategy to lift problems into a graded algebra whenever possible.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_5_.28Structural_Proof_without_polynomials.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_(Structural_Proof_without_polynomials)\">Solution 5 (Structural Proof without polynomials)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 (Structural Proof without polynomials)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>This is the same proof strategy as Solution 4, but without the polynomial machinery, and doesn't rely on unstated theorems of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Cyclotomic_polynomial\" title=\"Cyclotomic polynomial\">cyclotomic polynomials</a>, and proves the hard part of the problem: the interval lemma that Solution 1 proves but Solution 4 merely states.\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/b/e2bbebb3bd73f1ae5c64098ab0244f739abf7ca4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$U$\" width=\"14\" height=\"12\" /> be the expression (not the numerical value) given in the problem, with the numerator and denominator separated:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/5/4b5b08aa9f88d6483651f5b32d9b4ba1794f0267.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[U = \\frac{(k^2)! \\cdot \\prod_{j=0}^{k-1} j!}{\\prod_{j=0}^{k-1} (j+k)!}\\]\" width=\"148\" height=\"55\" />\nLet <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/d/e7dc47c501c5655496e911d25778a45b2b71390f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M_E(d)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"54\" height=\"20\" /> represents the <i>net count</i> of multiples of an integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ab646de7704969b91c76a214126b45f2b07b25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> in an multiplicative expression <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> (not a number itself!)\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/4/90434d79b536eac0396a62772b0dd81802157514.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[M_U(d) = \\left( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{k^2}{d} \\right\\rfloor + \\sum_{j=0}^{k-1} \\left\\lfloor \\frac{j}{d} \\right\\rfloor \\right) - \\sum_{j=0}^{k-1} \\left\\lfloor \\frac{j+k}{d} \\right\\rfloor\\]\" width=\"344\" height=\"54\" />\n</p><p><br />\nTo prove <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is an integer, it suffices to show that for every prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, the exponent of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> in the prime factorization of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, denoted <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/6/a76da1de6bd39ffa59b05d6d0f98d4c3762adae1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_p(U)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"47\" height=\"20\" />, is non-negative.\n</p><p>Equivalently, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/6/ad63be01390849237e89c6556bd26243cc2ab1be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_p(\\text{numerator(U)}) \\ge _p(\\text{denominator(U)})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"296\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p><b>Step 1: Decomposing the Valuation</b>\n</p><p>By Legendre's Formula, the exponent of a prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/f/63f344e7d8b14a7886fc7fb72da53cd0a3271dcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/e/f6eb98df3ae93ceb0b36891881d04bc017f8e56f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_p(n!) = \\sum_{i=1}^{\\infty} \\lfloor \\frac{n}{p^i} \\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"133\" height=\"53\" />. However, we won't sum these directly. Instead, we each prime power <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/6/5961a34f1d146caddb87089f240db3fdef4fb421.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p^i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"17\" height=\"21\" /> separately count the number of factors that are multiples.\n</p><p>Applying this to our expression, the total exponent <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/6/a76da1de6bd39ffa59b05d6d0f98d4c3762adae1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_p(U)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"47\" height=\"20\" /> is the sum of unit contributions from each power of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/f/59fcecf0925d9225326e6596ebfab076bb7c043e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[v_p(U) = \\sum_{i=1}^{\\infty} M(p^i)\\]\" width=\"144\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p><b>Step 2: The Structural Inequality</b>\n</p><p>Instead of analyzing the infinite sum for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/6/a76da1de6bd39ffa59b05d6d0f98d4c3762adae1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_p(U)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"47\" height=\"20\" /> directly, we will prove a stronger condition: that <b>each individual term</b> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/7/5a7f4c463567142ef49c43ebb30246a0969ca673.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M_U(d)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"54\" height=\"20\" /> is non-negative for any integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/3/ac3a335b38758f03b02a3ee280098ad9d9288a77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d \\ge 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"45\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p><p>We rewrite the sum terms in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/7/5a7f4c463567142ef49c43ebb30246a0969ca673.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M_U(d)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"54\" height=\"20\" />:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/b/0eb5b8063b77424a1e9d0836aff61352b1c9858d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sum_{j=0}^{k-1} \\left\\lfloor \\frac{j}{d} \\right\\rfloor - \\sum_{j=0}^{k-1} \\left\\lfloor \\frac{j+k}{d} \\right\\rfloor  = - \\sum_{j=0}^{k-1} \\left( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{j+k}{d} \\right\\rfloor - \\left\\lfloor \\frac{j}{d} \\right\\rfloor \\right)\\]\" width=\"393\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>Thus, the expression for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/7/5a7f4c463567142ef49c43ebb30246a0969ca673.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M_U(d)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"54\" height=\"20\" /> becomes:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/1/bf14840d24977cfa9bde7c0b158d39f7cad4e1f7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[M_U(d) = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{k^2}{d} \\right\\rfloor - \\sum_{j=0}^{k-1} \\left( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{j+k}{d} \\right\\rfloor - \\left\\lfloor \\frac{j}{d} \\right\\rfloor \\right)\\]\" width=\"313\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p><b>Step 3: Algebraic Verification</b>\nLet <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/b/9cb6ffeb21957a6f7b1f7c87ac8a73b558b86adc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = qd + r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"85\" height=\"18\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/e/6be7172b4ad8fc81493ddd176020590a55cfe1d6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 \\le r &lt; d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"78\" height=\"17\" />. \n</p><p>The term inside the summation, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/0/a50176aeec9734dacaad87bd5622dec316538f8c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lfloor \\frac{j+k}{d} \\rfloor - \\lfloor \\frac{j}{d} \\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"115\" height=\"39\" />, counts how many multiples of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ab646de7704969b91c76a214126b45f2b07b25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> lie in the half-open interval <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/3/7c3dcd0c77fd6370dfb6f97018bad7b71ee0b29d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(j, j+k]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"70\" height=\"20\" />. \nSince the interval has length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />, this count is either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> (if no <i>extra</i> multiple fits) or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/1/111cf4cbc2306ff9a43cbf46abf1da81cedd3e12.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"39\" height=\"15\" /> (if an extra multiple fits).\n</p><p>Specifically, the <i>extra</i> multiple (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/1/111cf4cbc2306ff9a43cbf46abf1da81cedd3e12.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"39\" height=\"15\" />) occurs exactly when the interval <i>wraps around</i> a multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ab646de7704969b91c76a214126b45f2b07b25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> in the remainder arithmetic. Thus:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/2/ca212695e7b3a8f1550dc0d4331899f1197b2c6e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sum_{j=0}^{k-1} \\left( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{j+k}{d} \\right\\rfloor - \\left\\lfloor \\frac{j}{d} \\right\\rfloor \\right)= k \\cdot q + \\max(0, 2r - d)\\]\" width=\"368\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/7/5a7f4c463567142ef49c43ebb30246a0969ca673.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M_U(d)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"54\" height=\"20\" />'s leading term <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/6/4662008380034808be2e3c4039de52789574b1b3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lfloor \\frac{k^2}{d} \\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"39\" height=\"41\" /> is:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/9/159a90140ec5a1c35ace0e3268e21cb19af61296.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\left\\lfloor \\frac{k^2}{d} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{(qd+r)^2}{d} \\right\\rfloor = q^2 d + 2qr + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{r^2}{d} \\right\\rfloor = k \\cdot q + qr + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{r^2}{d} \\right\\rfloor\\]\" width=\"474\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>Thus the condition <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/2/762fef4de47dbb679adf52d47cfb39ed90f51f41.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left[M_U(d) = \\left(k \\cdot q + qr + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{r^2}{d} \\right\\rfloor\\right) -  \\left(k \\cdot q + \\max(0, 2r - d) \\ge 0\\right)\\right]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"497\" height=\"45\" /> is equivalent to:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/f/3cfbe14a4b7311aed3d8f264ee3dbf9d65173cc3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[qr + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{r^2}{d} \\right\\rfloor \\ge \\max(0, 2r - d)\\]\" width=\"217\" height=\"43\" />\nWe analyze two cases:\n</p>\n<ul><li><b>Case 1 (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/2/492ea07757184392647517d62012acd234cf966c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2r \\le d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"53\" height=\"17\" />):</b> The RHS is 0. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/f/73f7305af3417879ac35e9d7f14ada2911e052d4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q, r, d \\ge 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"18\" />, the LHS is clearly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/9/aa9c151a661a687f6e3e9b6e95ff672c231b6ef3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ge 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"30\" height=\"16\" />. The inequality holds.</li></ul>\n<ul><li><b>Case 2 (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/0/8d0a4519a529d4c5150969bd4efefce4b8adaa06.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2r &gt; d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"53\" height=\"15\" />):</b> The RHS is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/d/39de8daa574ecea9e99b7cb72def984a0d1c7521.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2r - d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"16\" />. We need to show <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/6/466b33fad15172ea83faa18af63e66f44c2c6b1e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lfloor \\frac{r^2}{d} \\rfloor \\ge 2r - d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"112\" height=\"41\" />.</li></ul>\n<p>Since the LHS is an integer, this is equivalent to showing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/b/e5ba613ebcdc03172643703a5a66783528bfacb6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{r^2}{d} \\ge 2r - d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"96\" height=\"41\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/3/223b1456a5e0974ee6d286117de5ba18e85f2d60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r^2 \\ge 2rd - d^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"109\" height=\"19\" />. Rearranging gives:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/8/5486bb19429d755dec91275d0fd7b1c24ab91c0e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(r - d)^2 \\ge 0\\]\" width=\"93\" height=\"20\" />\nThis is the Trivial Inequality.\n</p><p><br />\n<b>Step 4: Conclusion</b>\n</p><p>We have proven that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/a/eba97340f676c095fde72b644bc2b88d5eed4ad3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M(d) \\ge 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"78\" height=\"20\" /> for <b>every</b> integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ab646de7704969b91c76a214126b45f2b07b25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Therefore, for any prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, the total valuation is a sum of non-negative terms:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/4/1c47b201408af51945e5e0f1bf36a8291618d7cf.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[v_p(N) = \\sum_{i=1}^\\infty M(p^i) \\ge 0\\]\" width=\"181\" height=\"48\" />\nSince <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/f/3af0e631eb821cfd89916a4e65199d1dc64ced27.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_p(N) \\ge 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"82\" height=\"20\" /> for all primes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is an integer.\n</p><p>~~oinava / Gemini 3 Pro\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_USAMO\" title=\"2016 USAMO\">2016 USAMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_USAMO_Problems\" title=\"2016 USAMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=27&amp;year=2016\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2016 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">Problem 1</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2016 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">Problem 3</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2016 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2016 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2016 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2016 USAMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_USAMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2016 USAMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018000003\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.055 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.752 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 677/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 978/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 48/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 24893/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    7.759      1 Template:USAMO_newbox\n100.00%    7.759      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:14955:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018000003 and revision id 275033. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}